artifact.self_contained_problem:
Let H be a rotationally scalar (i.e., SO(3)-invariant) many-body Hamiltonian acting on the antisymmetric Fock space of A nucleons. Consider the variational problem of minimizing the energy E[D] = Tr(HD) over many-body density operators D that are themselves rotational scalars (i.e., commute with the total angular momentum operators). For any such scalar D, the associated one-body density ρ(r⃗) = ⟨r⃗|ρ̂|r⃗⟩ depends only on r = |r⃗|, so it reduces to a monopole radial profile ρ₀(r), r ∈ [0, ∞), with ∫₀^∞ ρ₀(r) 4πr² dr = A. By a constrained Hohenberg–Kohn-type reduction, the minimization yields a scalar energy functional F₀[ρ₀] of the radial profile alone.

Formulate precise mathematical criteria, expressed solely in terms of features of the radial profile ρ₀ (such as its slope ρ₀'(R) at the nuclear surface radius R, higher derivatives at the surface, or radial moments ⟨rⁿ⟩ = ∫₀^∞ rⁿ ρ₀(r) 4πr² dr / A, n ∈ ℝ), that distinguish among the following classes of nuclei:

(a) spherical versus (axially or otherwise) deformed nuclei;
(b) "hard" (rigid surface) versus "soft" (fluctuating surface) nuclei;
(c) triaxial nuclei;
(d) halo nuclei.

In particular, prove or disprove the conjecture that the magnitude of the surface slope |ρ₀'(R)| is strictly smaller for deformed nuclei than for spherical ones with comparable mass and charge, and identify the minimal set of functionals of ρ₀ whose values determine membership in each of the classes (a)–(d).

